[1mNAME[0m
       [1mclear [22m- clear the terminal screen

[1mSYNOPSIS[0m
       [1mclear [22m[[1m-x[22m] [[1m-T [4m[22mterminal-type[24m]

       [1mclear -V[0m

[1mDESCRIPTION[0m
       [1mclear  [22mclears  your  terminal's  screen and its scrollback buffer, if any.  [1mclear [22mretrieves the terminal type from the environment variable
       [4mTERM[24m, then consults the [4mterminfo[24m terminal capability database entry for that type to determine how to perform these actions.

       The capabilities to clear the screen and scrollback buffer are named “clear” and “E3”, respectively.  The latter is a [4muser-defined[24m [4mcapabil‐[0m
       [4mity[24m, applying an extension mechanism introduced in [4mncurses[24m 5.0 (1999).

[1mOPTIONS[0m
       [1mclear [22mrecognizes the following options.

       [1m-T [4m[22mtype[24m  produces instructions suitable for the terminal [4mtype[24m.  Normally, this option is unnecessary, because the terminal type is inferred
                from the environment variable [4mTERM[24m.  If this option is specified, [1mclear [22mignores the environment variables  [4mLINES[24m  and  [4mCOLUMNS[24m  as
                well.

       [1m-V       [22mreports the version of [4mncurses[24m associated with this program and exits with a successful status.

       [1m-x       [22mprevents [1mclear [22mfrom attempting to clear the scrollback buffer.

[1mPORTABILITY[0m
       Neither IEEE Std 1003.1/The Open Group Base Specifications Issue 7 (POSIX.1-2008) nor X/Open Curses Issue 7 documents [1mclear[22m.

       The  latter  documents [1mtput[22m, which could be used to replace this utility either via a shell script or by an alias (such as a symbolic link)
       to run [1mtput [22mas [1mclear[22m.

[1mHISTORY[0m
       A [1mclear [22mcommand using the [4mtermcap[24m database and library appeared in 2BSD (1979).  Eighth Edition Unix (1985) later included it.

       The commercial Unix arm of AT&T adapted a different BSD program ([1mtset[22m) to make a new command, [1mtput[22m, and replaced the [1mclear [22mprogram  with  a
       shell script that called “[1mtput clear[22m”.

           /usr/bin/tput ${1:+-T$1} clear 2> /dev/null
           exit

       In 1989, when Keith Bostic revised the BSD [1mtput [22mcommand to make it similar to AT&T's [1mtput[22m, he added a [1mclear [22mshell script as well.

           exec tput clear

       The remainder of the script in each case is a copyright notice.

       In 1995, [4mncurses[24m's [1mclear [22mbegan by adapting BSD's original [1mclear [22mcommand to use [4mterminfo[24m.  The [1mE3 [22mextension came later.

       •   In June 1999, [4mxterm[24m provided an extension to the standard control sequence for clearing the screen.  Rather than clearing just the vis‐
           ible part of the screen using

               printf '\033[2J'

           one could clear the scrollback buffer as well by using

               printf '\033[[1m3[22mJ'

           instead.  “XTerm Control Sequences” documents this feature as originating with [4mxterm[24m.

       •   A few other terminal emulators adopted it, such as PuTTY in 2006.

       •   In  April 2011, a Red Hat developer submitted a patch to the Linux kernel, modifying its console driver to do the same thing.  Documen‐
           tation of this change, appearing in Linux 3.0, did not mention [4mxterm[24m, although that program  was  cited  in  the  Red  Hat  bug  report
           (#683733) motivating the feature.

       •   Subsequently,  more terminal developers adopted the feature.  The next relevant step was to change the [4mncurses[24m [1mclear [22mprogram in 2013 to
           incorporate this extension.

       •   In 2013, the [1mE3 [22mcapability was not exercised by “[1mtput clear[22m”.  That oversight was addressed in 2016 by reorganizing [1mtput [22mto  share  its
           logic with [1mclear [22mand [1mtset[22m.

